# ============================================================================
# NEVER commit these — they either contain runtime data, secrets, or are
# machine-generated. If you're not sure whether something should be on this
# list, err on the side of adding it here rather than committing it.
# ============================================================================

# The actual database file (demo/dev SQLite). In production you're on SQL
# Server anyway, but if you ever run the demo mode from this checkout,
# never let this file reach GitHub — it can contain real user records,
# document metadata, and password hashes.
gmpdoctrack.db
*.db
*.db-journal

# Python runtime artifacts
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.Python
*.egg-info/
venv/
.venv/

# Uploaded company logo / any future file uploads — these belong to your
# specific deployment, not the shared codebase.
app/static/uploads/*
!app/static/uploads/.gitkeep

# Environment / secrets files, if you ever create one
.env
.env.*
*.local.bat
*.local.sh

# These two files get edited with real secrets in place (a real SQL
# Server password, a real session-secret value) as part of normal setup
# — see docs/Installation_Guide_Complete.md. That means the discipline
# has to happen at commit time, not just from a filename pattern: before
# every commit, check "git status" or GitHub Desktop's changed-files
# list and make sure neither of these ever gets committed once it holds
# a real secret. Both files carry an explicit warning comment for this
# same reason. If you ever want the extra safety of gitignore actually
# catching it, save your edited copy under a name matching this pattern
# instead of editing the tracked file in place (e.g.
# set_db_env.PRODUCTION.bat) — but either way, checking before you
# commit is what actually protects you.
deployment/windows/start_server.*.bat
!deployment/windows/start_server.bat
deployment/windows/set_db_env.*.bat
!deployment/windows/set_db_env.bat

# OS/editor noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
